"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8890],{8890:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"pharmacology-test-2","slug":"pharmacology","subject":"Pharmacology","testNo":2,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 35-year-old female, a known case of sickle disease came with complaints of unprovoked pain in the extremities and torso. A thromboembolic episode was suspected. What is the first drug to be started for prophylaxis of thromboembolic events in this patient?","options":["Warfarin","Dabigatran","Aspirin","Heparin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Heparin\\n\\nThe clinical scenario is suggestive of a sickle cell crisis. This patient with unprovoked pain in the extremities and torso most likely has a thromboembolic episode. The first drug to be started for prophylaxis of thromboembolic events is heparin.\\n\\nThe mainstay of prophylaxis of thromboembolic events lies in anticoagulant medication. The initial parenteral therapy with heparin, LMWH, or fondaparinux shifted to a novel oral anticoagulant like dabigatran with warfarin acting as a bridge drug, followed by a long oral monotherapy with rivaroxaban or apixaban for a few weeks.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":2,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following is a penicillinase-resistant penicillin?","options":["Penicillin V","Cloxacillin","Benzathine penicillin","Ampicillin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloxacillin\\n\\nCloxacillin\xa0is penicillinase-resistant penicillin.\\n\\nPenicillinase is a beta-lactamase enzyme that inactivates penicillin\xa0by destroying the beta-lactam ring. This enzyme is produced by most staphylococci, some gonococci strains, Bacillus subtilis, E.coli, Hemophilus influenza etc..,\\n\\nPenicillinase-resistant penicillins are not inactivated by penicillinase. But, they are less efficient than penicillin G against penicillin-sensitive pathogens. They are used to treat staphylococcal infections that are sensitive to methicillin.\xa0Penicillinase-resistant penicillins are:\\n\\n• Cloxacillin\\n• Dicloxacillin\\n• Flucloxacillin\\n• Methicillin\\n• Nafcillin\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Penicillin V is an acid-resistant penicillin preparation that is administered orally. But it has limited use against penicillinase-producing organisms.\\n\\nOption C: Benzathine penicillin G and procaine penicillin G are repository preparations of penicillin G and are long-acting. They are given as deep I.M. injections. Benzathine penicillin has the longest half-life and is administered every 2-4 weeks. It is sensitive to penicillinase.\\n\\nOption D: Ampicillin\xa0is an extended-spectrum penicillin but is not resistant to beta-lactamases.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":3,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following statements about methotrexate is false?","options":["It targets the S-phase of the cell cycle","It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase","Non-proliferative cells are resistant to it","Folinic acid enhances its action"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Folinic acid enhances its action\\n\\nFolinic acid,\xa0also known as\xa0leucovorin, the active form of folic acid,\xa0is administered with methotrexate to reduce the toxicity associated with higher doses. It does not enhance the action of methotrexate.\\n\\nMethotrexate is an antimetabolite that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase.\xa0It is a cell cycle (S-phase) specific antineoplastic agent and non-proliferative cells are relatively resistant to it.\\n\\nBone marrow suppression and\xa0gastrointestinal mucosa inflammation (mucositis) are the common side effects seen due to inhibition of DNA synthesis.\\n\\nMethotrexate toxicity:\\n\\n• Prevention -\xa0administration of leucovorin/folinic acid.\\n• Treatment -\xa0maintaining adequate hydration and forced alkaline diuresis in patients with normal renal function.\\n• Glucarpidase\xa0is an intravenous drug administered in patients\xa0who have\xa0impaired renal function.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":4,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A young patient was found to have triglyceride levels of 600 mg/ dL. His LDL levels are slightly lower than normal and HDL levels are reduced. His mother too has elevated triglycerides with reduced levels of LDL and HDL cholesterol. The patient appears healthy, has a normal BMI, and has no history of coronary heart disease in his family. Which of the following drugs will be beneficial for this patient?","options":["Fenofibrate","Colestipol","Cholestyramine","Ezetimibe"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Fenofibrate\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario, the patient has elevated levels of triglycerides with low levels of LDL and HDL cholesterol. This pattern is also seen in his mother. Therefore, it is most likely to be familial hypertriglyceridemia.\xa0Since the patient does not have risk factors for coronary heart disease, fenofibrate will be beneficial in reducing triglyceride levels.\\n\\nFamilial hypertriglyceridemia occurs due to a defect in the lipolysis of the triglyceride-rich lipoproteins (TRLs). As a result of this, the levels of fasting triglyceride levels are elevated. Plasma LDL levels are reduced due to the defective conversion of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins to LDL. Identification of hypertriglyceridemia in first-degree relatives helps in the diagnosis.\\n\\nHypertriglyceridemia can also be secondary due to\xa0intake of simple carbohydrates, obesity, insulin resistance, alcohol use, and estrogen treatment.\\n\\nInitial management involves exercise and dietary management (restriction of fatty food, avoidance of alcohol, estrogen-containing medications, etc)\\n\\nIf the levels of the triglycerides are >500 mg/dL despite lifestyle changes, then fibrates and fish oil can be used to reduce the levels and decrease the risk of pancreatitis.\xa0If the patient has risk factors for coronary heart disease, then statin therapy will be beneficial.\\n\\nClass\\nHMG- CoA reductase inhibitors (statins)\\nFibrates\\nNiacin\\nBile acid-binding resins\\nEzetimibe\\n\\nImportant examples\\n\\nLovastatin, atorvastatin, fluvastatin, pravastatin, simvastatin, rosuvastatin, pitavastatin\\n\\nGemfibrozil and fenofibrate\\n\\nNiacin\\n\\nColestipol, cholestyramine, and colesevelam\\n\\nEzetimibe\\n\\nMechanism of action\\n\\nInhibits HMG-CoA reductase\\n\\nPPAR-α mediated increased lipolysis\\n\\nDecreases the production of VLDLs and decreases the catabolism of HDLs\\nincrease the excretion of bile acids. This results in increased conversion of cholesterol mobile acids by the liver\\ninhibits intestinal sterol absorption\\n\\nMajor effects on lipoprotein levels\\n\\nReduces LDL levels\\n\\nReduces triglyceride levels\\n\\nA moderate increase in HDL levels\\n\\nReduces triglycerides,\xa0 LDL, and lipoprotein a levels\\n\\nSignificant increase in HDL levels\\n\\nReduces LDL levels\\n\\nReduces LDL cholesterol\\n\\nMinimal increase in HDL level\\n\\nAlso decreases the absorption of phytosterols\\n\\nImportant side effects\\n\\nElevations of serum aminotransferase activity\\n\\nElevation of creatine kinase activity\\n\\nMyopathy\\n\\nrashes, gastrointestinal symptoms, myopathy, arrhythmias, hypokalemia, elevated aminotransferases or alkaline phosphatase\\n\\nCutaneous vasodilation\\n\\nElevations in aminotransferases levels\\n\\nConstipation\\n\\nBloating\\n\\nreversible hepatic function impairment\\n\\nRarely myositis","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Contraceptive failure while on oral contraceptive pills is unlikely to occur with which of the following drugs?","options":["Rifampicin","Valproate","Phenobarbitone","Phenytoin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Valproate\\n\\nContraceptive failure in patients on the oral contraceptive pill is unlikely to occur with valproate. It is an enzyme inhibitor that inhibits the degradation of OCPs and increases their duration of action.\\n\\nRifampicin, phenytoin, and phenobarbitone are enzyme inducers and can cause increased metabolism of OCPs leading to contraceptive failure.\\n\\nEnzyme inducers\\nEnzyme inhibitors\\n\\nMnemonic: CRAP GPS\\n\\n• Carbamezepine\\n• Rifampicin\\n• Alcohol (chronic)\\n• Phenytoin\\n• Griseofulvin\\n• Phenobarbitone\\n• Sulfonylureas\\n\\nMnemonic:\xa0RED VICK\\n\\n• Ritonavir\\n• Erythromycin\\n• Diltiazem\\n• Verapamil,\xa0Valproate\\n• Itraconazole\\n• Clarithromycin,\xa0Cimetidine\\n• Ketoconazole","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":6,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient started on an antihypertensive presented with the following picture. Which of the following drugs is the most likely cause?","options":["Lisinopril","Nifedipine","Atenolol","Clonidine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lisinopril\\n\\nThe image shows edema over the lips and face and its occurrence following intake of an antihypertensive is suggestive of drug-induced angioedema. Lisinopril, an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor (ACE inhibitor), is most likely\xa0responsible for this condition.\\n\\nAngioedema with ACE inhibitors:\\n\\nIn very few patients(0.1%-0.5%), ACE inhibitors cause rapid swelling\xa0in the nose, throat, mouth, glottis, larynx, lips, or tongue.\xa0Once the\xa0drug is stopped\xa0symptoms improve drastically over hours. Angioedema of the intestine can occur very rarely.\\n\\nACE inhibitor-associated angioedema\xa0is a\xa0class effect, and patients who develop this adverse event should\xa0not be prescribed\xa0any other drugs within the ACE inhibitor class.\\n\\nOther adverse effects of ACE inhibitors:\\n\\n• A sudden fall in blood pressure can occur following the first dose of ACE inhibitors.\\n• It can produce a bothersome dry cough due to the accumulation of bradykinin, substance P, or prostaglandins in the lungs. The cough usually disappears within 4 days of the stoppage of the drug.\\n• Hyperkalemia due to K+ retention can occur.\\n• Acute renal failure can occur with ACE inhibitors in patients with\xa0bilateral renal artery stenosis, stenosis of the artery to a single remaining kidney, heart failure,\xa0or volume depletion due to\xa0diarrhea or diuretics.\\n• It should be discontinued in pregnant women as it can cause developmental defects.\\n• It can also cause maculopapular skin rash in some cases.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Nifedipine is a calcium channel blocker. Common adverse effects include headache, flushing, and dizziness. Peripheral edema (pedal edema) may rarely occur.\\n\\nOption C: Atenolol is a beta-1 selective antagonist. Bradycardia, fatigue, and cold extremities are common adverse effects. CNS side effects like nightmares and depression are less frequent with atenolol than other beta-blockers.\\n\\nOption D: Clonidine is an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist. The main adverse effects of clonidine are dry mouth and sedation. Bradycardia and sexual dysfunction are other effects.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/09a1390422ec31579444.jpg"]},{"n":7,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Identify the drugs marked as A, B, and C.","options":["A - Aspirin, B -Vorapaxar, C - Prasugrel","A - Aspirin, B - Eptifibatide, C - Vorapaxar","A - Aspirin, B - Eptifibatide, C - Prasugrel","A - Aspirin, B - Prasugrel, C - Eptifibatide"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A - Aspirin, B - Eptifibatide, C - Vorapaxar\\n\\nThe drugs marked A, B and C are aspirin, eptifibatide, and vorapaxar,\xa0respectively.\\n\\nAspirin causes inhibition of cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1),\xa0which would inhibit the formation of thromboxane A2. Thromboxane A2 is an inducer of platelet aggregation and is a potent vasoconstrictor. Hence, inhibition of its formation is responsible for antiplatelet action.\\n\\nAspirin is a non-competitive, irreversible inhibitor of the cyclooxygenase (COX) enzyme. It is a nonselective COX inhibitor and inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2.\\n\\nAbciximab, tirofiban, and eptifibatide act by\xa0blocking Gp IIb/IIIa receptors. They are mainly used during\xa0percutaneous coronary intervention\xa0for acute coronary syndrome. They carry a\xa0high risk of bleeding\xa0and can cause\xa0thrombocytopenia.\\n\\nVorapaxar\xa0primarily inhibits platelet aggregation caused by\xa0thrombin.\xa0PAR-1 activation by thrombin contributes to platelet aggregation. Vorapaxar is a competitive antagonist of protease-activated receptor-1 (PAR-1). Vorapaxar is\xa0indicated in combination\xa0with aspirin and/or clopidogrel to reduce thromboembolic events in patients with MI or peripheral vascular disease.\\n\\nPrasugrel\xa0is an\xa0irreversible P2Y12\xa0inhibitor and lasts for the entire lifetime of a platelet.\\n\\nPrasugrel is a\xa0prodrug\xa0that is used in patients with acute coronary syndrome who have undergone\xa0percutaneous coronary intervention.\xa0It\xa0reduces\xa0the rate of\xa0thrombotic cardiovascular events\xa0in such patients.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/18351d6bb88dc1d5502f.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0956c19bb8a754a1422e.jpg"}]},{"n":8,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Cardiotoxicity is an adverse effect of which of the following anti-cancer drugs?","options":["Cladribine","Cyclophosphamide","Doxorubicin","Tamoxifen"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Doxorubicin\\n\\nCardiotoxicity is a unique adverse effect of doxorubicin. It is an anthracycline group of anticancer antibiotics.\\n\\nAnthracyclines:\\n\\nThey work by interacting with DNA directly and\xa0affecting transcription and replication. They have the potential to\xa0create free radicals\xa0which cause\xa0irreversible cardiotoxicity\xa0and\xa0cardiomyopathy\xa0which is dose-dependent.\\n\\nAdverse effects:\\n\\n• Cardiomyopathy\xa0is the most common adverse effect and it can be either\xa0acute,\xa0in the form of\xa0myocardial damage(\\"pericarditis-myocarditis syndrome\\"),\xa0or a\xa0chronic\xa0form of\xa0dose-dependent cardiomyopathy\xa0leading to heart failure.\\n• Alopecia, anemia, amenorrhea, leukopenia, nausea, vomiting.\\n• Mucositis, diarrhea, thrombocytopenia.\\n• Less common: acute lymphoid leukemia,acute myelogenous leukemia,atrioventricular block,esophagitis,hyperpigmentation,myelodysplastic syndrome\\n\\nAnthracycline-induced cardiomyopathy takes two forms:\\n\\n• Acute:\xa0pericarditis-myocarditis syndrome,\xa0arrhythmias, ST and T wave changes in ECG.\\n• Chronic: dilated cardiomyopathy.\\n\\nAnti-cancer drugs and their toxicities:\\n\\nDrug\\nToxicity\\n\\nCyclophosphamide\xa0& Ifosfamide\\nHemorrhagic cystitis, SIADH\\n\\nBusulfan\\nPulmonary fibrosis\\n\\nProcarbazine\\n\\nSecondary leukemias\\n\\nDisulfiram-like reaction\\n\\nCisplatin\\n\\nNephrotoxicity\\n\\nNeurotoxicity\\n\\nOtotoxicity\\n\\nCapecitabine\\nDiarrhea, hand-foot syndrome,\\nmyelosuppression\\n\\nCytarabine\\nCerebellar ataxia\\n\\nIrinotecan\\nDiarrhea\\n\\nBleomycin\\nPulmonary fibrosis\\n\\nAnthracyclines\\nCardiotoxicity\\n\\nMitomycin\\nHemolytic-uremic syndrome\\n\\nCladribine\\nMyelosuppression, nausea\\nand vomiting, and\\nimmunosuppression\\n\\nTamoxifen\\nEndometrial cancer, osteoporosis","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":9,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Postural hypotension is a side effect of which of the following classes of drugs?","options":["Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) Inhibitors","Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARB)","Thiazide diuretics","Alpha blockers"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Alpha blockers\\n\\nDrug-induced orthostatic/postural hypotension is\xa0caused by the effect of alpha-blocker (alpha1\xa0- selective receptor blocker).\\n\\nAlpha1 receptor blockers, such as prazosin, cause a reduction of peripheral resistance as well as venous pooling of blood. This causes a decrease in venous return and cardiac output leading to a fall in blood pressure. Postural reflex causes marked postural hypotension which leads to syncope. This dizziness and fainting are known as the\xa0first dose effect\xa0and can be minimized by starting with a minimal dose and titrating it thereafter. Other side effects include reflex tachycardia, miosis, nasal congestion, and impotence.\\n\\nThey are used as an antihypertensive agent and in benign hypertrophy of the prostate to decrease prostatic tone.\\n\\nThe\xa0prostate stroma\xa0has a maximum concentration of\xa0α1A\xa0receptors over other α1\xa0receptor subtypes.\xa0α1B\xa0receptors are present on\xa0blood vessels\xa0which play a role in\xa0vasoconstriction.\xa0Hence, selective\xa0α1A\xa0receptor antagonists have minimal effect on blood pressure as compared to non-selective α1\xa0antagonists like prazosin.\\n\\nα1\xa0receptor antagonists can be classified as:\\n\\n• Selective antagonists\\n\\n• Silodosin (α1A)\\n• Tamsulosin (α1A\xa0+ α1D)\\n\\n• Non-selective\\n\\n• Prazosin\\n• Terazosin\\n• Doxazosin\\n• Alfuzosin\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Side effects of Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) Inhibitors include - dry cough, hyperkalemia, angioedema, renal Impairment\\n\\nOption B: Side effects of Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers (ARB) - hyperkalemia, dizziness\\n\\nOption C: Side effects of thiazide diuretics include- hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperuricemia, hyperglycemia","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":10,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"What is the mechanism of action of the drug allopurinol?","options":["Converts uric acid into allantoi","Inhibition of cyclooxygenase enzyme","Increase uric acid excretion","Xanthine oxidase inhibitor"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Xanthine oxidase inhibitor\\n\\nAllopurinol is recommended as the first-line urate-lowering agent in all patients with chronic gout. It is a\xa0purine antimetabolite and an inhibitor of the xanthine oxidase enzyme.\\n\\nIt prevents the conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid reducing plasma uric acid levels. Allopurinol is also a substrate for xanthine oxidase. The metabolite of allopurinol called oxypurinol is a non-competitive inhibitor of xanthine oxidase.\\n\\nFebuxostat\xa0is a uric acid synthesis inhibitor that acts by\xa0inhibiting\xa0the enzyme\xa0xanthine oxidase\xa0and it is indicated in gout management.\\n\\nBoth Febuxostat and Allopurinol are Xanthine Oxidase inhibitors, of which Allopurinol is a hypoxanthine analog.\\n\\nDrugs used in the management of gout can be classified as:\\n\\nDrugs that\xa0relieve inflammation and pain- These drugs are useful in the management of\xa0acute attacks of gout. They are:\\n\\n• NSAIDs:\xa0They mainly exert their antiinflammatory action by\xa0inhibiting cyclooxygenase enzyme\xa0(COX).\xa0This enzyme converts arachidonic acid to\xa0prostanoids, thromboxane A2, and other prostaglandins\\n• Colchicine:\xa0It arrests the cell division by interfering with microtubule and spindle formation. This results in decreased neutrophil recruitment decreased neutrophil motility and decreased release of chemotactic factors.\\n• Glucocorticoids:\xa0These drugs bind to the nuclear receptors and decrease the transcription of genes for\xa0COX-2, inducible NOS (NOS2), inflammatory cytokines, collagenase, etc, that are responsible for inflammation.\\n\\nDrugs that\xa0inhibit urate formation- These drugs inhibit urate formation by inhibiting the enzyme xanthine oxidase. Eg. allopurinol and febuxostat.\xa0The most common adverse effect of allopurinol is\xa0hypersensitivity reactions.\xa0Oxypurinol\xa0is a metabolite of allopurinol and inhibits xanthine oxidase thus reducing uric acid production. It is an\xa0orphan drug\xa0and is used in patients intolerant to allopurinol.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Pegloticase and rasburicase catalyze the enzymatic oxidation of uric acid into allantoin. This product is soluble and inactive.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0NSAIDs:\xa0They mainly exert their antiinflammatory action by\xa0inhibiting cyclooxygenase enzyme\xa0(COX).\xa0This enzyme converts arachidonic acid to\xa0prostanoids, thromboxane A2, and other prostaglandins\\n\\nOption C: Uricosuric drugs: These drugs increase the excretion of uric acid. Important ones are:\\n\\n• Probenecid-\xa0it\xa0inhibits the reabsorption of uric acid by inhibiting organic acid transporter (OATs), principally URAT-1.\\n• Benzbromarone- It increases uric acid secretion by\xa0reversibly inhibiting urate–anion exchanger in the proximal tubule\\n• Lesinurad- It\xa0inhibits the URAT-1 and OAT-4 transporters, thereby reducing renal uric acid reabsorption.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":11,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 60-year-old female patient presented with the following finding. Her medical history includes hypertension for which she has been taking regular medications for the past 3 months. Which of the following medication is most likely to be associated with this finding?","options":["Enalapril","Telmisartan","Amlodipine","Hydrochlorthiazide"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Amlodipine\\n\\nThe image shows pitting pedal edema in a hypertensive patient. Calcium channel blockers (CCBs) like amlodipine are most likely to be associated with peripheral edema.\\n\\nThe frequency of CCB- induced peripheral edema is both drug-specific (i.e more potent drugs like amlodipine will be associated with higher rates of developing edema) and dose-dependent (i.e incidence of edema increases with an increase in drug dosage and chronicity of its usage). It is more common among females.\\n\\nCCB-induced peripheral edema occurs due to a disproportionate decrease in the\xa0arteriolar resistance than in the venous circulation, resulting in increased\xa0hydrostatic pressure in the precapillary circulation. This leads to a fluid shift\xa0into the interstitial compartment causing edema.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Dose reduction.\\n• Interclass switching of CCB - switch to non-dihydropyridines like verapamil, and diltiazem.\\n• Addition of a venodilator - ACE inhibitor, ARBs, Nitrates.\\n• Others - compression stockings, avoiding prolonged upright position.\\n• Definitive treatment would be discontinuation of the CCB and switching to another class of antihypertensives.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Common side effects of ACE inhibitors like enalapril include hypotension, hyperkalemia, dry cough, rashes, urticaria, dysgeusia, gastrointestinal upset, headache, and dizziness.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Telmisartan, an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARBs), causes adverse effects such as hypotension, hyperkalemia, headache, gastrointestinal upset, and dizziness.\\n\\nOption D: Adverse effects of thiazide diuretics like hydrochlorothiazide are hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia, hypercalcemia, rashes,\xa0headache, gastrointestinal upset, and dizziness.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/71006fb7671c69dc66fc.jpg"]},{"n":12,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"What is the mechanism of action of exenatide, that is used in the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus?","options":["It is a DPP-4 inhibitor","It is a GLP 1 analogue","It inhibits SGLT-2","It is an amylin analogue"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. It is a GLP 1 analogue\\n\\nExenatide is a GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) receptor agonist. It stimulates insulin secretion, inhibits glucagon release, delays gastric emptying, reduces food intake, and normalizes fasting and postprandial insulin secretion. It is given subcutaneously. It can also cause pancreatitis, and therefore is not used in patients with a history or risk of developing pancreatitis.\\nGLP-1 analogs are peptides and are generally given other than oral routes due to degradation by intestinal peptidases. But semaglutide was synthesized by adding sodium caprylate which increases its permeability and leads to its absorption before it gets degraded. Hence, it enabled oral usage of a GLP-1 analog.\\nMechanism of Action:\xa0GLP-1 receptor agonists stimulate glucose-dependent insulin secretion from pancreatic β-cells, suppress glucagon secretion from pancreatic α-cells, and delay gastric emptying.\\n\\nGLP-1 analogs: Exenatide ,Liraglutide ,Albiglutide ,Dulaglutide, Lixisenatide ,Semaglutide\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Linagliptin\xa0is a\xa0dipeptidyl-peptidase-4\xa0(DPP-4)\xa0inhibitor. It is used in type 2 diabetes as monotherapy or in combination therapy when metformin is not tolerated or contraindicated. It\xa0binds extensively to plasma proteins and is cleared\xa0primarily by the hepatobiliary system.\xa0Hence, no dosage adjustment is needed in renal failure.\\n\\nOption C: Canagliflozin is a\xa0sodium-glucose transporter 2\xa0(SGLT-2)\xa0inhibitor. Other SGLT-2 inhibitors include dapagliflozin and empagliflozin. They act by\xa0inhibiting the\xa0SGLT-2 transporter which is present on the\xa0proximal\xa0segment of the\xa0renal tubule. It is responsible for the reabsorption of 80-90% of filtered glucose.\xa0It\xa0inhibits glucose reabsorption resulting in glycosuria and a change in the renal threshold.\\n\\nOption D: Pramlintide\xa0is an islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP) or amylin analog.\xa0Amylin analogs reduce glucagon secretion, slow gastric emptying, and centrally decrease appetite.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":13,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"An elderly male patient is diagnosed with benign prostatic hypertrophy. The physician starts him on tamsulosin. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":["Selective α1 receptor antagonism","Selective α1 receptor agonism","Nonselective α receptor antagonism","Selective α2 receptor agonism"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Selective α1 receptor antagonism\\n\\nTamsulosin acts by selective\xa0α1\xa0receptor\xa0antagonism.\\n\\nTamsulosin\xa0is\xa0a uroselective α1\xa0blocker that\xa0acts by decreasing the tone of prostatic/ bladder neck muscles. This reduces the dynamic obstruction and increases the urinary flow rate. It is used in the treatment of BPH and acute urinary retention. It is taken once a day and preferred in cases with postural hypotension as it has very little effect on BP.\\n\\nRetrograde ejaculation\xa0and floppy iris during cataract surgery are the side effects of this drug.\\n\\nOther α1\xa0antagonists used in BPH include\\n\\n• Terazosin\\n• Doxazosin\\n• Alfuzosin\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0\xa0Selective α1 receptor agonists such as phenylephrine are\xa0used as nasal decongestants and also as a mydriatic agent.\xa0Midodrine\xa0has been used to treat patients with autonomic insufficiency and postural hypotension.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Nonselective α receptor antagonism can be seen in drugs such as\xa0phenoxybenzamine and phentolamine. They are administered prior to the surgical removal of pheochromocytoma to control the episodes of severe hypertension. They have also been used to manage the cheese reaction occurring in patients who take MAO inhibitors.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Selective α2\xa0receptor agonists such as clonidine have been used in treating hypertension.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":14,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"While working as a resident in a hospital, you are asked by the consultant to prescribe a certain proton-pump inhibitor (PPI). It should be given only orally once daily before breakfast, even though it has a half-life of no more than 3 hours. What is the rationale behind this once-daily dosing?","options":["The drug is gradually released irrespective of its formulation.","The drug irreversibly inactivates the pump molecule.","The drug suppresses basal but not stimulated acid production.","The drug can inhibit all gastric pumps."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. The drug irreversibly inactivates the pump molecule.\\n\\nThe rationale behind the once-daily dosing is that the drug\xa0irreversibly inactivates the pump molecule.\\n\\nProton-pump inhibitors (PPIs) are prodrugs that are activated only after undergoing an acid-catalyzed cleavage in the canaliculi of the parietal cells of the stomach.\\n\\nOn administration, they are absorbed in the proximal small bowel and eventually, block the\xa0H+/K+ ATPase enzyme, the \\"proton pump\\" in the parietal cells of the stomach. The secretion of protons by an activated H+/K+- ATPase (proton pump) in the parietal cells into the gastric lumen is the final step involved in the production of gastric acid\xa0and inhibition of this enzyme prevents acid secretion into the stomach.\\n\\nOnce PPIs irreversibly inactivate the H+K+-ATPase pump, acid secretion resumes only after new pump molecules are synthesized and inserted into the luminal membrane. This leads to prolonged suppression of acid secretion for up to 24-48 hours, despite having a shorter plasma half-life of only 30 minutes to 3 hours.\\n\\nIn most individuals, once-daily dosing is enough to achieve effective acid inhibition. If necessary, a second dose can be given before an evening meal.\\n\\nThe number of H+K+-ATPase pump molecules increases after fasting and food decreases the oral bioavailability of the drug. Hence, PPIs are given around 30 minutes before the first meal of the day.\\n\\nPPIs cannot inhibit all gastric acid pumps with oral dosing because not all pumps are active during the short half-life of the PPI in the blood.\\n\\nPPIs cause maximum suppression of gastric acid. They reduce the daily production of both basal and stimulated acid secretion by 85–90%.\\nThere are available in the form of delayed-release tablets, capsules, and oral suspension packets and for immediate acid suppression, there are intravenous formulations for PPIs like lansoprazole, pantoprazole, and esomeprazole. Therefore, the release of the drug depends upon the drug formulation.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/33797d1393d9a11ae17b.png"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 20-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department with complaints of breathing difficulty, facial swelling, and palpitations following a bee sting. He was immediately given an intramuscular injection of a drug in 1:1000 dilution. The drug acts on which of the following receptor to improve the patient\'s breathing difficulty?","options":["Beta 2","Alpha 1","Beta 1","Alpha 2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Beta 2\\n\\nThe patient\'s complaints of\xa0breathing difficulty, facial swelling, and palpitations following a bee sting are suggestive of anaphylactic shock. The drug\xa0of choice for anaphylaxis is intramuscular adrenaline (0.5ml in 1:1000 dilution). Activation of beta 2 receptors causes bronchodilation. Hence, adrenaline action on the beta 2 receptors improves the breathing difficulty in this patient. Apart from this, beta 2 receptors also inhibit the secretion from mast cells.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nReceptor\\nAction\\nUse in anaphylaxis treatment\\n\\nAlpha 1 and alpha 2\\nVasoconstriction\\nIncrease in BP\\n\\nBeta 1\\nCardiac stimulation\\nIncrease in BP\\n\\nBeta 2\\nBronchodilation\\nReverse bronchoconstriction\\n\\nAdrenaline acts on α1, α2, β1, β2, and β3 receptors.\\n\\n• α1 - present in blood vessels - causes vasoconstriction - increases BP.\\n• β2 - present in blood vessels - causes vasodilatation - decreases BP.\\n\\nAdrenergic drug\\n\\nReceptors that it acts on\\n\\nAdrenaline\\n\\nα1 + α2\xa0+β1 + β2 + weak β3 action\\n\\nNoradrenaline\\n\\nα1 +α2 +β1 + β3 but no β2 action\\n\\nIsoprenaline\\n\\nβ1 + β2 + β3 but no α action","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"What is the mechanism of action of doxycycline?","options":["Cell wall synthesis inhibitor","Protein synthesis inhibitor","DNA gyrase inhibitor","Leucyl-tRNA synthetase inhibitor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Protein synthesis inhibitor\\n\\nDoxycycline is a tetracycline that acts by inhibiting protein synthesis.\\n\\nProtein synthesis Inhibitors:\\n\\nAntibacterial drugs which bind to the\xa030s\xa0subunit of the 70s ribosome are given below:\\n\\n• Tetracyclines\\n• Aminoglycosides\\n\\nAntibacterial drugs which bind to\xa0the 50s\xa0subunit of 70s ribosome are given below:\\n\\n• Macrolides\\n• Clindamycin\\n• Streptogramins\\n• Chloramphenicol\\n• Linezolid\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A:\xa0Cefotetan and oxacillin are beta-lactam antibiotics that act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone that acts by inhibiting DNA gyrase.\\n\\nOption D: Tavaborole inhibits fungal leucyl-tRNA synthetase, thereby inhibiting fungal protein synthesis and finally resulting in fungal cell death.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"The given image shows the synthesis and release of GABA in the nerve terminal. Identify the drug that acts at site A.","options":["Benzodiazepines","Tiagabine","Baclofen","Gabapentin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Baclofen\\n\\nThe drug that acts at the site \'A\' is baclofen.\\n\\nBaclofen is a\xa0centrally-acting muscle relaxant.\xa0It is an analog of the inhibitory transmitter GABA and acts as a selective\xa0GABAB\xa0receptor agonist. It also acts at the spinal cord where it decreases excitatory transmitter release of excitatory neurotransmitter release.\\n\\nBaclofen is used for the treatment of\xa0spasticity\xa0in multiple sclerosis, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, spinal injuries, and flexor spasm. In these conditions, baclofen is the preferred drug for symptomatic relief.\\n\\nIt can also be administered intrathecally around the spinal cord through a catheter placed surgically. Side effects of baclofen include drowsiness, mental confusion, weakness, and ataxia.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Benzodiazepines are allosteric modulators of the GABAA\xa0receptor that bind at the\xa0α/γ interface\xa0of the receptor. This\xa0increases\xa0the\xa0affinity\xa0of the GABAA\xa0receptor for\xa0GABA\xa0thus\xa0increasing\xa0the frequency\xa0of the\xa0opening\xa0of the chloride\xa0channel. They are selective CNS depressants that produce sedation, relieve anxiety, facilitate sleep, suppress seizures, and reduce muscle tone.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Tiagabine inhibits\xa0the\xa0GABA transporter GAT-1 and thereby reduces GABA uptake into neurons and glia and prolongs the action of GABA in the synapse. It is also used as an adjunct therapy for partial seizures with or without secondary generalization.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Gabapentin\xa0is an anti-epileptic drug that acts by binding to α2δ protein. It is used in focal onset seizures, neuropathic pains like\xa0post-herpetic neuralgia,\xa0and\xa0neuropathic pain.\xa0The most common adverse effects seen with gabapentin are\xa0increased drowsiness, ataxia,\xa0and\xa0fatigue.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/1a9225a079e6e885739c.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a23eaf71ef3216cec14e.jpg"}]},{"n":18,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following drugs come under Schedule H of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1945?","options":["Biologicals and special products like insulin, toxin and antitoxins","Bacterial vaccines","Drugs purchased with the prescription of a qualified doctor","Drugs with a warning label “Caution: it is dangerous to take this preparation except under medical supervision”"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Drugs purchased with the prescription of a qualified doctor\\n\\nSchedule H includes drugs purchased over the counter with the prescription of a qualified doctor.\\n\\nThe Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, classify drugs under different schedules based on their potential for abuse, their therapeutic value, and other factors. The rules provide guidelines for the storage, sale, display, and prescription of each schedule.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A- Biological and special products like insulin, toxin, and antitoxins are included under\xa0Schedule C.\\n\\nOption B- Bacterial vaccines are included under\xa0Schedule F I.\\n\\nOption D- Drugs that should be labelled with the words “Caution: it is dangerous to take this preparation except under medical supervision” are included under\xa0Schedule G.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":19,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A farmer presents to the emergency department with diarrhea, sweating, excessive lacrimation, and salivation. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the treatment of the condition?","options":["Pralidoxime has a role in reversing the enzyme in carbamate poisoning","Atropine is used as an antidote for OP poisoning","Carbamates bind to acetylcholinesterases reversibly","Succinylcholine use is avoided in rapid sequence intubation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Pralidoxime has a role in reversing the enzyme in carbamate poisoning\\n\\nThe\xa0clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0organophosphate\xa0poisoning. Pralidoxime is used to counter the\xa0nicotinic\xa0effects of organophosphates. It\xa0reactivates\xa0the\xa0acetylcholinesterase,\xa0which\xa0had been deactivated in organophosphate poisoning.\\n\\nAtropine\xa0is useful in\xa0organophosphate poisoning\xa0because it is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine.\xa0An acetylcholinesterase enzyme has an active and anionic site. The\xa0organophosphates\xa0bind to\xa0the active\xa0site of AChEs\xa0(acetylcholine esterases) and\xa0deactivate\xa0it. This results in excessive Ach causing excessive muscarinic activity. It has relatively insignificant action on nicotinic receptors.\\n\\nOrganophosphates and carbamates are powerful acetylcholinesterase\xa0inhibitors. While carbamates bind reversibly, organophosphates bind irreversibly.\\n\\nThe predominant initial signs are of\xa0muscarinic\xa0excess which includes:\xa0(DUMBBELSS)\\n\\n• Diarrhea\\n• Urination\\n• Miosis\\n• Bronchospasm\\n• Bradycardia\\n• Emesis\\n• Lacrimation\\n• Salivation\\n• Sweating\\n\\nNicotinic manifestations\xa0include twitching of eyelids, tongue, and facial muscles which then leads to paralysis.\xa0CNS stimulation\xa0results in headaches, tremors, ataxia, coma, and death.\\n\\nPatients who present with organophosphate and carbamate poisoning require early endotracheal intubation to avoid respiratory depression, which is a common complication. Succinylcholine is avoided due to its metabolism by acetylcholinesterase which leads to prolonged neuromuscular blockade.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":20,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"High-ceiling diuretics are used in the treatment of all of the following conditions except:","options":["Generalised edema","Cerebral edema","Acute pulmonary edema","Pulmonary hypertension"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Pulmonary hypertension\\n\\nHigh-ceiling diuretics\xa0or loop diuretics are not used in pulmonary hypertension.\\n\\nTheir diuretic property helps in removing the excess fluid in hepatic, renal, and cardiac diseases and their vasodilatory and diuretic action is helpful for the quick relief of symptoms in LVF and acute\xa0pulmonary edema. They are also used for the treatment of chronic CHF.\\n\\nPulmonary artery hypertension\xa0is a condition where there is increased pulmonary vascular resistance. Since nitric oxide is an important regulator of pulmonary vascular resistance, PDE5 inhibitors like sildenafil, vardenafil, or tadalafil are primarily used in the treatment of pulmonary arterial hypertension.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Generalized edema occurs due to fluid retention in systemic tissues, commonly seen in conditions like heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, or chronic kidney disease. High-ceiling diuretics are effective here as they can rapidly reduce extracellular fluid volume by promoting diuresis.\\n\\nOption B: Although loop diuretics are not the first-line treatment for cerebral edema, they may be used in conjunction with other therapies like mannitol to reduce intracranial pressure by decreasing overall fluid volume\\n\\nOption C: Loop diuretics help reduce pulmonary congestion by decreasing preload and promoting diuresis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":21,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient scheduled for a CABG procedure receives preoperative heparin for anticoagulation. During the surgery, the patient starts bleeding massively and there is a drop in BP. Which of the following would you use to manage this patient?\\n\\nCABG: Coronary Artery Bypass Graft","options":["Aminocaproic acid","Protamine sulfate","Prothrombin complex concentrate","Tranexamic acid"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Protamine sulfate\\n\\nProtamine sulfate is the drug used to treat life-threatening, heparin-induced bleeding.\\n\\nIt is a mixture of basic polypeptides isolated from salmon sperm.\xa0It binds to acidic heparin with high affinity and neutralizes anticoagulant activity. The resultant protamine-heparin complexes are cleared. Protamine binds only to long heparin molecules (UFH). Therefore, protamine only partially reverses\xa0the activity of LMWH and has no effect on\xa0fondaparinux.\\n\\nIt is most commonly used to neutralize heparin-induced anticoagulation after separation from cardiopulmonary bypass. It is also utilized in the setting of dialysis, invasive vascular procedures, and acute ischemic strokes.\\n\\nNote: Protamine sulfate has anticoagulant activity at higher doses and hence, only small amounts of protamine (1mg/100 units of heparin) are used for heparin-induced bleeding.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following is not an orphan drug?","options":["Miltefosine","Acetaminophen","Succimer","Sodium nitrite"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Acetaminophen\\n\\nAcetaminophen is not an orphan drug. It is an essential drug.\\n\\nOrphan drugs are drugs for rare diseases.\\n\\nOrphan drugs are for the treatment or prevention of rare diseases with little commercial value for pharmaceutical companies. Therefore, special incentives are given by the government for the pharmaceutical companies for the development and marketing of orphan drugs.\\n\\nFew examples of orphan drugs:\\n\\n• Miltefosine\\n• Sodium nitrite\\n• Succimer\\n• Rifabutin\\n• Liposomal amphotericin B\\n• Fomepizole","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":23,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A drug with a half-life of 8 hours is administered orally three times a day. How long will it take for the plasma concentration to reach a steady state?","options":["36 hours","30 hours","16 hours","24 hours"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 36 hours\\n\\nThe time taken to reach steady-state plasma concentration is 4-5 half-lives. As the half-life of the given drug is 8 hours, it will take 32-40 hours to reach a steady state. Hence, 36 hours\xa0will be the most appropriate answer.\\n\\nSteady-state:\xa0The amount of drug administered is equal to the amount of drug eliminated within one dosing interval resulting in a plateau or constant drug plasma concentration, with the plasma concentration fluctuating between a minimum and maximum.\\n\\nThe time taken to reach steady-state plasma concentration is 4-5 half-lives.\\n\\nDrugs with a shorter half-life reach a steady state rapidly, whereas drugs with a longer half-life take days to weeks to attain a steady state.\\n\\nSteady-state concentration (Css) = F x Dosing rate/ CL\\n\\n• where the dosing rate is the dose per time interval and is dose/T\\n• F is the fractional bioavailability, CL is clearance.\\n\\nIn the case of intravenous infusion,\\n\\nSteady-state concentration (Css) = Rate of infusion/ CL\\n\\nPharmacokinetics of repeated drug dosing:\\n\\nPlasma concentration of the drug will increase, reach a maximum, and decline after the first dose. A second dose will be administered before the first dose is eliminated. Consequently, the plasma concentration resulting from the second dose will be higher than from the first dose.\\n\\nThis increase in plasma concentration of the drug will occur continuously on repeated dosing (drug accumulation occurs) until a steady state at which drug elimination is equal to drug administration is reached. This is known as the plateau principle of drug accumulation.\\n\\nA graph is plotted between plasma drug concentration and time as shown below. The given graph depicts the plateau principle of drug accumulation on repeated oral dosing.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/d5d06705576a5f3a2e20.jpg"}]},{"n":24,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient undergoing chemotherapy was given an antiemetic, after which he developed symptoms like acute dystonia, bradykinesia, and tremors. Which of the following drugs would have caused these symptoms?","options":["Ondansetron","Metoclopramide","Meclizine","Scopolamine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Metoclopramide\\n\\nIn the above scenario, the patient has developed\xa0extrapyramidal adverse effects like acute dystonia, bradykinesia, and tremors. This is likely due to\xa0the\xa0antagonist action on dopamine receptors by metoclopramide.\\n\\nMetoclopramide is a prokinetic and antiemetic. It has the following pharmacological actions:\\n\\n• Dopamine receptor antagonist\\n• Serotonin (5HT4) receptor agonist\\n• Serotonin (5HT3) antagonist\\n• Sensitization of muscarinic receptors on smooth muscle. This results in increased lower esophageal sphincter tone and stimulates gastric antral contractions.\\n\\nMetoclopramide acts on both the gastrointestinal system and central nervous system. It is used as an upper antiemetic for many types of vomiting, especially migraine, drug-induced, and chemotherapy-induced. It is also used as a prokinetic in gastroparesis to increase gastric emptying.\\n\\nAdverse effects of metoclopramide are mainly extrapyramidal effects, and these include acute dystonia, Parkinson-like symptoms (tremors, bradykinesia, rigidity), tardive dyskinesia, galactorrhea due to hyperprolactinemia, and methemoglobinemia in neonates.\\n\\nExtrapyramidal symptoms can be treated by discontinuation of metoclopramide and administration of anticholinergics or antihistaminics.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Ondansetron is a\xa05-HT3 receptor antagonist. It blocks impulses at the peripheral origin in GIT. It is most commonly indicated for the treatment of\xa0postoperative\xa0and chemotherapy-induced\xa0nausea and vomiting. Headache and dizziness are the common adverse effects.\\n\\nOption C: Meclizine is a\xa0long-acting H1 antihistaminic drug.\xa0It is used for seasickness. It is less sedative and less anticholinergic.\\n\\nOption D: Scopolamine (hyoscine) is a muscarinic antagonist. It blocks neural pathways from the vestibular apparatus in the inner ear to the emetic center in the brainstem. A transdermal patch of scopolamine is used as an antiemetic in motion sickness. Constipation, drowsiness, dry mouth, and blurred vision are the common adverse effects.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":25,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 30-year-old female patient with early morning stiffness and chronic pain over small joints was diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis. She has been on drugs for the past few years, without any improvement. Now, it has been decided to start her on biological agents. Which of the following will not be used in this patient?","options":["IL-17A antagonist","IL-1 receptor antagonist","IL-6 receptor antagonist","TNF-alpha antagonist"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. IL-17A antagonist\\n\\nIL-17A antagonist\xa0is not used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis.\\n\\nIL-17A antagonists\xa0(secukinumab, ixekizumab) can be used in the treatment of moderate to severe plaque or scalp psoriasis.\\n\\nAll the other 3 are\xa0biological Disease Modifying Anti-Rheumatoid Drugs\xa0(DMARDs) used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis. All biological DMARDs downregulate the immune system and can lead to adverse effects like opportunistic infections and the flaring of latent TB.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0IL-1 receptor antagonist - anakinra\\n\\nOption C:\xa0IL-6 receptor antagonist - tocilizumab and sarilumab\\n\\nOption D:\xa0TNF\xa0α antagonist - adalimumab, golimumab, etanercept, etc.,","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":26,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following drugs is effective in the delayed phase of post-chemotherapy nausea and vomiting?","options":["Aprepitant","Domperidone","Granisetron","Metoclopramide"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Aprepitant\\n\\nAprepitant is the drug preferred for delayed onset vomiting after cancer chemotherapy.\\n\\nNausea and vomiting associated with chemotherapy occur in two phases:\\n\\n• The acute phase is seen within 24 hours and occurs in almost all patients receiving chemotherapy. In response to chemotherapeutic agents, the enterochromaffin cells in the small intestine secrete serotonin, which acts on the 5HT3 receptors and causes vomiting. This phase is treated by 5HT3 antagonists\xa0such as ondansetron, granisetron, and palanosetron.\\n\\n• The delayed phase is seen on days 2-5 and occurs only in some patients. It occurs in response to stimulation of NK-1 receptors by substance P. 5HT3 antagonists are not effective against the delayed phase, and NK-1 receptor antagonists\xa0are used in these patients.\\n\\nNK-1 receptor antagonists include aprepitant and its parenteral formulation, fosaprepitant, rolapitant, and netupitant. These drugs are usually administered in combination with 5HT3 antagonists and dexamethasone for highly emetogenic chemotherapy. A fixed-dose combination of netupitant and palanosetron has been approved to cover both the acute and delayed phase of post-chemotherapy nausea and vomiting.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":27,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"The drug of choice for the management of hyperthyroidism in the third trimester of pregnancy is?","options":["Propylthiouracil","Lugol\'s iodine","Methimazole","Levothyroxine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Methimazole\\n\\nThe drug of choice for the management of hyperthyroidism in the third trimester of pregnancy\xa0is methimazole.\\n\\nHyperthyroidism in pregnancy:\\n\\nPre-pregnancy and first trimester: Propylthiouracil\\n\\n2nd, 3rd trimester + post-pregnancy:\\n\\n• Methimazole\\n• Partial thyroidectomy (in 2nd trimester if\xa0methimazole is not effective)\\n\\nMethimazole and its prodrug (carbimazole) are usually avoided in the first trimester due to\xa0teratogenic risk.\xa0However, methimazole is used for the remainder of the pregnancy due to the concern for\xa0PTU-associated liver failure.\\n\\nSubtotal thyroidectomy\xa0can be performed during the\xa0mid-trimester.\\n\\nRadioactive iodine\xa0crosses the placenta and causes the destruction of the fetal thyroid. Therefore, it\xa0is\xa0contraindicated\xa0during pregnancy and lactation.\\n\\nPropranolol will help with her heart rate (is the patient is symptomatic), but will not help with the overactive thyroid.\\n\\nIf a pregnant woman is on propylthiouracil in her first trimester, she should be switched to methimazole in her 2nd trimester.\\n\\nNote (in pregnancy):\\n\\n• Lowest possible dose should be prescribed\\n• Radioiodine therapy contraindicated\\n\\nTrimester\\nTreatment for thyrotoxicosis\\n\\nFirst trimester\\n\\n• Propylthiouracil\\n\\nSecond trimester\\n\\n• Methimazole\\n• Subtotal thyroidectomy\\n\\nThird trimester\\n\\n• Methimazole\\n\\nAbnormalities associated with methimazole (when used in 1st trimester):\\n\\n• Choanal atresia\\n• Aplasia cutis\\n• Fetal goiter\\n• Esophageal atresia\\n• Scalp defects","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":28,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which among the following is the most potent opioid analgesic from the options given below?","options":["Morphine","Fentanyl","Pentazocine","Pethidine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Fentanyl\\n\\nAmong the given options, fentanyl is the most potent opioid.\\n\\nOpioid potency:\\n\\nSufentanil > Fentanyl > Morphine > Pentazocin and pethidine.\\n\\nNote: Overall, Sufentanil is the most potent opioid. It is 1000 times more potent than morphine.\\n\\nOther high-yield points on opioids:\\n\\n• Post-thoracotomy pain managed by: IV fentanyl\\n• Drug of choice for controlling severe pain in cancer patients: Morphine\\n• Drug of choice for OPD analgesia: Alfentanyl\\n• Most potent: Sufentanyl\\n• Shortest-acting opioid: Remifentanyl\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Fentanyl is around 100 times more potent than morphine.\\n\\nOptions C & D: The potency of pentazocin and pethidine is lower than that of morphine","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Gynecomastia is an adverse effect of which of the following drugs?","options":["Spironolactone","Both A and B","Furosemide","Amiloride"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Spironolactone\\n\\nSpironolactone has an additional affinity towards progesterone and androgen receptors resulting in gynecomastia, loss of libido, menstrual irregularities, and other adverse effects.\\n\\nEplerenone has a lower affinity to progesterone and androgen receptors due to its 9,11 epoxide group. The incidence of gynecomastia and other effects are minimal with eplerenone.\\n\\nOther options: Amiloride and furosemide are not associated with gynecomastia","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":30,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Arrange the following formulations of amphotericin B (AMB) according to the increasing order of nephrotoxicity.","options":["Conventional-AMB < Liposomal amphotericin B < Amphotericin B lipid complex","Liposomal amphotericin B < Amphotericin B lipid complex < Conventional-AMB","Conventional-AMB < Amphotericin B lipid complex < Liposomal amphotericin B","Liposomal amphotericin B < Conventional-AMB < Amphotericin B lipid complex"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Liposomal amphotericin B < Amphotericin B lipid complex < Conventional-AMB\\n\\nThe correct order of nephrotoxicity with amphotericin B formulations is liposomal amphotericin B < amphotericin B lipid complex < conventional-amphotericin B.\\n\\nNephrotoxicity with\xa0amphotericin B:\\n\\nAll lipid formulations of amphotericin B are less nephrotoxic than conventional amphotericin B.\xa0Liposomal amphotericin B is less nephrotoxic than amphotericin B lipid complex, especially in patients with a higher risk of nephrotoxicity. Azotemia is seen in 80% of cases with C-AMB. Renal tubular acidosis and renal wasting of potassium and magnesium are seen. Nephrotoxicity can be reduced by saline loading.\\n\\nOther adverse effects:\\n\\nInfusion-related reactions - It is most prominent with amphotericin B\xa0colloidal dispersion. Fever and chills following IV injection is a major acute reactions. Tachypnea, hypotension, or respiratory stridor may be also seen. Meperidine is used to shorten the reaction.\\n\\nHypochromic normocytic anemia may be seen due to decreased erythropoietin.\\n\\nHeadache, nausea, malaise, vomiting, and phlebitis at the site of injection is also seen.\\n\\nArachnoiditis may be seen with intrathecal injections.\\n\\nFormulations of amphotericin B:\\n\\nC-AMB(Conventional\xa0amphotericin B): It is formulated with bile salt deoxycholate and is given as an intravenous infusion.\\n\\nABCD(Amphotericin B colloidal dispersion): It is formed by equimolar concentrations of amphotericin B and cholesteryl sulfate.\\n\\nL-AMB(Liposomal\xa0amphotericin B): Here amphotericin B is incorporated within a small liposomal vesicle.\\n\\nABLC(Amphotericin B lipid complex): It is a complex of amphotericin B with two phospholipids.","img":false,"explImg":false}]}')}}]);